      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Health, Readiness and Personal Responsibility</h2>
            <p>Before you think about gear or technique, it helps to check in with yourself. Snorkeling is a relaxing way to explore the water, but it still asks something of your body and your judgment.</p>
            <p>This lesson covers two things: whether you are medically and physically ready, and what personal responsibility looks like once you are in the water. For the specific safety rules to follow once you have decided you are ready, see <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r08">Golden Rules for Safer Snorkeling</a>.</p>
            <h2>Medical Readiness</h2>
            <p>If you have a medical condition, a recent injury, or any concern about your fitness for physical activity in water, talk to a doctor before snorkeling. This matters even if the condition feels minor or unrelated to swimming, and it matters more, not less, as you get older: age itself raises the risk of the heart and lung issues that matter most in water, even without a diagnosed condition.</p>
            <p>Conditions that affect breathing, heart health, blood pressure, or consciousness deserve particular attention, since symptoms that are manageable on land can become dangerous in water. That includes everyday things you might otherwise shrug off: a cough that hasn't cleared up, getting more out of breath than usual doing ordinary activity, or chest tightness are all worth a doctor's opinion before you put that same body to work in the water. When in doubt, ask a medical professional rather than guessing.</p>
            <h2>A Simple Readiness Self-Check</h2>
            <p>Snorkeling does not require advanced athletic ability, but it does require a baseline level of comfort and competence in water. There's no official snorkeling-specific standard for this, but a widely used reference point comes from PADI's Open Water Diver course, which asks candidates to:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Swim 200 metres (300 metres in mask, fins, and snorkel) without stopping, no time limit, any stroke</li>
              <li>Float or tread water for 10 minutes, any method</li>
            </ul>
            <p>That's a scuba certification standard, more demanding than snorkeling strictly requires, but the diving industry treats it as a moderate, achievable bar for basic watermanship, not an elite one, which makes it a reasonable gut-check for snorkeling too. If you could comfortably do something in that range, you're at a solid baseline. If you're well short of it, that's useful information, not a failure, and swimming lessons are a practical way to close the gap before you snorkel in open water.</p>
            <p>These are reference points, not a certified snorkeling standard, and exact expectations can vary by provider and location.</p>
            <h2>What Personal Responsibility Means</h2>
            <p>Snorkeling is often a self-directed activity. Unlike a fully guided or supervised program, you may be responsible for your own decisions about when and where to enter the water, how long to stay in, and when to get out.</p>
            <p>Personal responsibility means:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Being honest with yourself about your fitness, comfort, and skill level</li>
              <li>Checking conditions before you enter, not assuming they will be fine</li>
              <li>Staying attentive to yourself and your surroundings the entire time you are in the water</li>
              <li>Knowing that no lesson, checklist, or piece of gear can guarantee your safety, only reduce risk</li>
            </ul>
            <p>This mindset connects directly to the <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r10">Pre-Snorkel Checklist and Planning</a> you should run through before every session, and to the buddy system covered in <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r14">Buddy Communication and Awareness</a>.</p>
          `,
