      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Surface Safety Devices</h2>
            <p>Good surface gear does double duty: it keeps you safe, and it takes the effort out of staying visible and afloat, so you can actually relax and enjoy what's below you instead of working at the surface. This lesson covers three common surface safety devices: the signaling buoy, the whistle, and the life jacket.</p>
            <p>Carrying a way to signal is also one of the core rules in <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r08">Golden Rules for Safer Snorkeling</a>; this lesson explains the specific devices in more detail. Choosing which devices to bring is also part of <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r10">Pre-Snorkel Checklist and Planning</a>.</p>
            <figure class="learning-media learning-media--gallery learning-media--compact"><div class="learning-media__gallery"><div class="learning-media__item"><div class="learning-media__image" style="--learning-media-aspect: 4 / 3;"><img src="data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///ywAAAAAAQABAAACAUwAOw==" alt="Bright orange signaling buoy used to make a snorkeler more visible at the surface." loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-learning-asset-filename="learning-safety-signaling-buoy.png" width="1100" height="825"></div><p class="learning-media__label">Signaling Buoy</p></div><div class="learning-media__item"><div class="learning-media__image" style="--learning-media-aspect: 4 / 3;"><img src="data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///ywAAAAAAQABAAACAUwAOw==" alt="Safety whistle used by a snorkeler to attract attention over water." loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-learning-asset-filename="learning-safety-whistle.png" width="1100" height="825"></div><p class="learning-media__label">Whistle</p></div><div class="learning-media__item"><div class="learning-media__image" style="--learning-media-aspect: 4 / 3;"><img src="data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///ywAAAAAAQABAAACAUwAOw==" alt="Orange life jacket that provides extra flotation support for snorkeling." loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-learning-asset-filename="learning-safety-life-jacket.png" width="1100" height="825"></div><p class="learning-media__label">Life Jacket</p></div></div><figcaption>A signaling buoy, whistle, and life jacket each solve a different surface-safety problem: visibility, attention, and flotation.</figcaption></figure>
            <h2>Signaling Buoy</h2>
            <p>A signaling buoy is a floating marker, often bright-colored, that trails behind you while you snorkel. It makes you more visible to boats and other snorkelers, and it can double as a flotation aid if you need to rest or hold onto something in open water.</p>
            <p>A signaling buoy is especially useful in areas with boat traffic, in open water away from a crowded beach, or whenever visibility to others matters more than usual.</p>
            <p>In some places this isn't just a good idea, it's the law. Florida, for example, legally requires a diver-down flag or buoy for anyone snorkeling with a mask and snorkel, not just scuba divers, boats must stay at idle speed within 300 feet of it in open water, and you're required to stay within that same distance of your own flag. Rules vary by location, check what applies where you're snorkeling.</p>
            <h2>Whistle</h2>
            <p>A whistle is small, lightweight, and effective at attracting attention. Sound travels well over water, and a whistle can help you signal for help or attention faster than shouting, especially over distance or in choppy conditions.</p>
            <p>A whistle is easy to carry, low effort to use, and worth having as a simple backup, even when you don't expect to need it.</p>
            <h2>Life Jacket</h2>
            <p>A life jacket provides buoyancy and flotation support. It is particularly useful for weaker swimmers or in more challenging conditions, where extra flotation reduces effort and adds a margin of safety.</p>
            <p>A life jacket is not only for weak swimmers, it is also a reasonable choice any time you want extra reassurance and reduced physical effort in the water, such as longer sessions or unfamiliar conditions.</p>
            <h2>Choosing What to Bring</h2>
            <p>Not every session calls for every device. Consider:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Are you snorkeling in an area with boat traffic? A signaling buoy is worth it.</li>
              <li>Are you in open water, away from a supervised or crowded area? A buoy and a whistle both help.</li>
              <li>Are you or your buddy a less confident swimmer, or expecting more challenging conditions? A life jacket adds a meaningful safety margin.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>When conditions or ability are uncertain, it is reasonable to bring more support rather than less.</p>
          `,
