      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Beach Flags and Surface Warnings</h2>
            <p>Many beaches use flags or posted signs to warn visitors about hazards, restricted areas, or local conditions. Recognizing that a flag system exists, and knowing to check what it means at your specific location, is one of the simplest safety habits you can build.</p>
            <p>This is not a lesson about currents or wave conditions themselves, see <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r12">Currents &amp; Rip Currents</a> for that. This lesson is about recognizing posted warnings.</p>
            <h2>Flag Systems Vary by Location</h2>
            <p>There is no single, worldwide flag system, and there isn't even one single system used at every beach within the United States. Colors, shapes, and meanings can differ by country, region, state, and individual beach authority. The United States Lifesaving Association (USLA) publishes a national reference system, but adopting it is a local decision, not a requirement everywhere, some states such as Florida have passed laws requiring standardized flags at public beaches, while other areas use their own local conventions.</p>
            <p>When you arrive at a new beach, look for posted signage explaining the local flag system, or ask a lifeguard or local authority if you are unsure.</p>
            <h2>Common Flag and Warning Conventions (United States)</h2>
            <p>The following match the USLA's published national reference system. Treat this as a general starting point, not a guarantee of what a specific beach's flags mean, always confirm locally.</p>
            <figure class="learning-media learning-media--diagram learning-media--educational-wide"><div class="learning-media__item"><div class="learning-media__image" style="--learning-media-aspect: 5 / 3;"><img src="data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///ywAAAAAAQABAAACAUwAOw==" alt="Diagram of common US beach warning flag examples including yellow, red, double red, purple, watercraft-area flags, and windsock guidance, with a reminder that local systems vary." loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-learning-asset-filename="learning-beach-warning-flags-us.svg" width="1200" height="720"></div><p class="learning-media__label">Common US / USLA Flag Examples</p></div><figcaption>Common US / USLA beach-warning examples. Local flag systems vary, so always read posted signage or ask a lifeguard.</figcaption></figure>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> Medium hazard, moderate surf and/or currents.</li>
              <li><strong>Red:</strong> High hazard, strong surf and/or currents.</li>
              <li><strong>Red over red (double red):</strong> Water is closed to the public.</li>
              <li><strong>Purple:</strong> Marine pests present, such as jellyfish or stingrays.</li>
              <li><strong>Red over yellow:</strong> A recommended swimming area with lifeguard supervision.</li>
              <li><strong>Black and white (checkered):</strong> Marks an area for surfboards and other non-powered watercraft.</li>
              <li><strong>Yellow with a black ball or symbol:</strong> Surfboards and other non-powered watercraft are prohibited.</li>
              <li><strong>Orange windsock:</strong> Offshore winds are present, inflatables should not be used.</li>
              <li><strong>Red and white (quartered):</strong> Emergency evacuation, swimmers should leave the water.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Again, confirm these locally. A flag that means one thing at one beach can mean something different, or nothing at all, somewhere else, even within the same country.</p>
            <h2>Snorkeling Abroad</h2>
            <p>If you snorkel outside the US, don't assume the flags above apply. There is no single global standard. Even the International Life Saving Federation, the closest thing to a global authority on this, states openly that a unified international system doesn't exist, some countries reject the red/yellow "lifeguarded area" flag outright, and some reject black-and-white flags for unrelated cultural reasons. As one example of real variation, several European countries add a green flag for calm, supervised conditions, a color the US system doesn't use at all, so don't assume a color's meaning, or its absence, carries over from what you know at home.</p>
            <p>The reliable way to find out what flags mean at your destination:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Ask your resort, dive shop, or tour operator, they'll know the local system.</li>
              <li>Look for posted signage at the beach itself, most systems are explained on-site.</li>
              <li>Ask a lifeguard or local authority directly if you're unsure.</li>
              <li>If you're researching before a trip, search for that country's national or regional lifesaving authority, similar to USLA in the US, rather than relying on general travel articles.</li>
            </ul>
          `,
