      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Currents &amp; Rip Currents</h2>
            <p>Water is always moving. Some movement is gentle and easy to manage. Some movement can affect where you enter, where you exit, and how easy it feels to navigate.</p>
            <p>This lesson focuses on current-related concepts: currents, longshore currents, tidal currents, and rip currents.</p>
            <figure class="learning-media learning-media--standard learning-media--wide"><div class="learning-media__item"><div class="learning-media__image" style="--learning-media-aspect: 700 / 450;"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0798/8055/2781/files/rip_from_above2_394617_398633.jpg?v=1788322608" alt="Aerial view of nearshore water showing a visible channel associated with rip-current movement." loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="700" height="450"></div></div></figure>
            <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
            <p>Currents can move you across the shore, away from where you planned to exit, or away from land. Recognizing moving water helps you make better decisions before entering. Rip currents caused the majority of the 47 surf zone fatalities recorded in the US so far this year, this isn't a theoretical risk.</p>
            <p>This is primarily a conditions-awareness lesson, with the one core response worth knowing if a current does catch you.</p>
            <h2>What Is a Current?</h2>
            <p>A current is a stream of water moving in a consistent direction. Currents are influenced by environmental factors and can affect both safety and navigation.</p>
            <p>In plain terms: a current is water with direction. If you ignore it, your planned route may not be the route you actually travel.</p>
            <h2>Three Current Types to Know</h2>
            <ol class="learning-rule-list">
              <li><strong>Longshore Currents</strong><span>For snorkelers, the main point is lateral movement. A longshore current may move you along the beach or coastline and affect your entry and exit points.</span></li>
            </ol>
            <figure class="learning-media learning-media--diagram learning-media--narrow"><div class="learning-media__item"><div class="learning-media__image" style="--learning-media-aspect: 1 / 1;"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0798/8055/2781/files/Longshore_current.png?v=1788322469" alt="Diagram showing a longshore current moving parallel to the shoreline." loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="500" height="500"></div><p class="learning-media__label">Longshore Current</p></div></figure>
            <ol class="learning-rule-list" style="--learning-rule-start: 2;">
              <li><strong>Tidal Currents</strong><span>This lesson treats tidal currents as a current-related concept. For a broader tide and sea-state view, see <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r13">Waves, Tides &amp; Surge</a>.</span></li>
            </ol>
            <figure class="learning-media learning-media--diagram learning-media--narrow"><div class="learning-media__item"><div class="learning-media__image" style="--learning-media-aspect: 1 / 1;"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0798/8055/2781/files/Tidal_currents.png?v=1788322468" alt="Educational diagram illustrating tidal water movement and the relationship between high and low tide." loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="500" height="500"></div><p class="learning-media__label">Tidal Currents</p></div></figure>
            <ol class="learning-rule-list" style="--learning-rule-start: 3;">
              <li><strong>Rip Currents</strong><span>For snorkelers and other water users, the key risk is direction: a rip current moves away from shore.</span></li>
            </ol>
            <figure class="learning-media learning-media--diagram learning-media--narrow"><div class="learning-media__item"><div class="learning-media__image" style="--learning-media-aspect: 1 / 1;"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0798/8055/2781/files/Rip_current.png?v=1788322470" alt="Diagram showing a rip current moving away from shore through the surf zone." loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="500" height="500"></div><p class="learning-media__label">Rip Current</p></div></figure>
            <h2>How to Spot a Rip Current</h2>
            <p>Rip currents can be hard to see, but there are real visual cues. Look for a channel where the waves aren't breaking the way they are on either side of it, or a patch of foam, seaweed, or discolored water moving steadily out past the surf line. If something about the water in front of you looks different from the water around it, treat that as a reason to look closer before entering.</p>
            <h2>Tidal Currents and Timing</h2>
            <p>Tides don't just change water depth, they also drive tidal currents, water moving in and out as the tide rises and falls. That movement is usually weakest during slack tide, the short window around high or low tide, and strongest in between. Aiming for slack tide is one of the simplest ways to reduce current risk, especially in channels, passes, and near points where currents run strong the rest of the time.</p>
            <p>For checking tide tables, marine forecasts, and how tide height itself affects entries and hazards, see <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r13">Waves, Tides &amp; Surge</a>.</p>
            <h2>Recognizing the Risk Before You Enter</h2>
            <p>Before entering, ask:</p>
            <ol>
              <li>Is water movement part of today's conditions?</li>
              <li>Could a current affect your navigation?</li>
              <li>Could a longshore current affect where you exit?</li>
              <li>Are there piers, jetties, or sandbar breaks nearby where rip currents may form?</li>
              <li>Are tidal currents likely to affect nearshore movement?</li>
              <li>Does any part of the water look different, a gap in breaking waves, foam, seaweed, or discoloration moving seaward?</li>
              <li>Have you checked the tide schedule and aimed for slack tide?</li>
            </ol>
            <h2>What to Understand Before Entering</h2>
            <p>You do not need to become an oceanographer to snorkel. You do need to notice that water movement can change the plan.</p>
            <p>Before entering, understand:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>currents have direction</li>
              <li>longshore currents can move you along the shore</li>
              <li>tidal currents can affect nearshore movement</li>
              <li>rip currents move directly away from shore</li>
              <li>structures and sandbar breaks may be associated with rip currents</li>
              <li>entry and exit planning should account for moving water</li>
            </ul>
            <h2>If You Get Caught in a Current</h2>
            <p>This lesson is mainly about awareness before you enter, but one response is too important to leave out entirely. If you're caught in a current moving you away from shore, do not swim directly against it, you will tire quickly and gain little ground. Stay afloat, signal for help, and swim parallel to the shore until you're free of the current, then swim back to shore at an angle. Once you're clear of the pull, it will not drag you under, it moves you out, not down.</p>
            <p>This is the single most important fact to know, not a substitute for a full rescue or emergency-response course.</p>
          `,
