      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Buddy Communication and Awareness</h2>
            <p>Snorkeling with a buddy is one of the simplest and most effective safety habits there is. A buddy can notice a problem before you do, get help quickly, and turn a small issue into something manageable instead of dangerous. It's also more fun, as you can point out fun fish species and enjoy the activity together.</p>
            <p>This lesson explains why the buddy system matters and how to set up simple communication with your buddy before you get in the water. The buddy rule itself is also listed in <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r08">Golden Rules for Safer Snorkeling</a>; this lesson goes into more detail on how to make it work in practice.</p>
            <h2>Why the Buddy System Exists</h2>
            <p>Underwater and at the surface, it can be hard to communicate normally, and it can be hard to notice your own condition changing. A buddy who is paying attention can spot signs of distress, fatigue, or disorientation that you might miss in yourself, and can act quickly, whether that means checking in, helping directly, or signaling for outside help.</p>
            <p>The buddy system only works if both people are actually paying attention to each other, not just swimming nearby.</p>
            <h2>Agree on Communication Before You Enter</h2>
            <p>Talking underwater is not possible with normal snorkel gear, so simple, agreed-upon signals matter. Before you get in the water, take a minute with your buddy to agree on:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>How you will signal that you are okay</li>
              <li>How you will signal that something is wrong</li>
              <li>How you will get each other's attention if needed</li>
              <li>What to do if you get separated</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The standard signal is a thumb-and-forefinger circle for "I'm okay." It's recognized across both snorkeling and diving, so it's the one to use. Skip thumbs up or thumbs down: in standard diving convention those specifically mean "let's ascend" and "let's descend," not a general good or bad check, and using them casually risks a real mix-up with a buddy who's trained to read them that way. Even with the OK circle, the most important thing is that you and your buddy actually confirm it means the same thing to both of you before you rely on it.</p>
            <h2>Staying Aware in the Water</h2>
            <p>Communication is not just about signals, it is also about attention. Keep a general sense of where your buddy is, check in periodically rather than only at the start and end of a session, and slow down or wait if you notice your buddy falling behind, struggling, or looking uncertain.</p>
            <p>If something feels off, whether with you or your buddy, treat that as worth pausing for. It is easier to check in early than to react to a problem that has already grown.</p>
          `,
