      title: "Duck Diving Basics",
      short_description: "Learn the basic technique for diving below the surface to get a closer look underwater.",
      lesson_type: "Skill lesson",
      category: 'in-water-skills',
      estimated_reading_time: "3-5 minutes",
      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Duck Diving Basics</h2>
            <p>Duck diving is how snorkelers descend below the surface for a closer look at the reef, a wreck, or anything else worth a nearer view. It's a breath-hold skill, which means the safety principles in <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r15">Breath-Hold Safety, CO2 &amp; Shallow-Water Blackout</a> apply directly here, read that lesson before practicing duck dives regularly.</p>
            <h2>Basic Technique</h2>
            <ol>
              <li>Start face down at the surface with your mask and snorkel in place, and take a normal, relaxed breath, not a forced or extended one.</li>
              <li>Bend at the hips, folding your upper body downward toward the bottom.</li>
              <li>Lift your legs up out of the water. Their weight helps drive your body downward as your hips continue the roll.</li>
              <li>Once submerged, kick calmly to continue your descent, keeping movements smooth rather than rushed.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>Some snorkelers remove the snorkel from their mouth just before descending and replace it once back at the surface, to avoid water entering if the tube floods. Others simply leave it in and clear it with the blast method on resurfacing, see <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r18">Clearing Your Snorkel</a>. Either approach is common; find what feels natural and confirm with an instructor if you're unsure which your local operator teaches.</p>
            <h2>Ascending and Resurfacing</h2>
            <p>Ascend calmly rather than rushing, sudden movement wastes energy and air. This isn't only about comfort, DAN's own data shows roughly 99% of blackouts happen at the surface or within about 5 metres of it, so the ascent and the first breaths after it deserve your full attention, not a rush to get back to diving.</p>
            <p>Before you break the surface, look up and check what's above you, boats, other swimmers, anything you couldn't see while your attention was on the dive. Keep one hand raised as you ascend so you're visible and can catch anything overhead before you're there, this matters most in areas with boat traffic. See <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r09">Surface Safety Devices</a> for equipment that helps you stay visible in the first place.</p>
            <p>Once you reach the surface, clear your snorkel if needed and breathe normally. Avoid immediately attempting another dive, give yourself time to breathe normally and recover first.</p>
            <h2>Buddy Safety While Duck Diving</h2>
            <p>Duck diving means one person is underwater while visibility and awareness are reduced. A good habit is for buddy pairs to alternate: one dives while the other stays on the surface watching, and that watch doesn't end the moment your buddy resurfaces. Keep watching for a full 30 seconds after they're back up, and confirm with a clear OK signal, not just a glance, blackout can still happen in those first breaths after surfacing. See <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r14">Buddy Communication and Awareness</a> for staying visible and aware of each other.</p>
          `,
