      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Buoyancy, Pressure &amp; Airspaces</h2>
            <p>A little basic science makes diving below the surface more comfortable and less confusing. This lesson covers three related ideas: why you float, why pressure increases as you go deeper, and why parts of your body need special attention as a result.</p>
            <h2>Buoyancy</h2>
            <p>Buoyancy is the force that lets you float. When you're in water, your body displaces water equal to its own volume, and the water pushes back with a force equal to that displaced weight. That upward push is what keeps you at the surface with relatively little effort. Saltwater is denser than freshwater, so it provides slightly more buoyant force, which is part of why floating usually feels easier in the ocean than in a pool.</p>
            <h2>Pressure and Depth</h2>
            <p>As you descend, water pressure increases because of the weight of the water above you. This matters because gas compresses under pressure: any air space in your body or gear shrinks as pressure rises, and expands again as you come back up. Even a few feet of descent is enough to notice this effect.</p>
            <h2>Airspaces in Your Body</h2>
            <p>Your body has several airspaces affected by this pressure change, most importantly your sinuses, middle ear, and lungs. As pressure increases with depth, these spaces compress. If they're not adjusted for, or "equalized," to match the surrounding pressure, this can cause discomfort or, in more serious cases, injury. This is why equalizing is a genuine skill worth learning properly rather than an optional detail, see <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r26">Equalizing Basics</a>.</p>
            <h2>Why This Matters When You Dive</h2>
            <p>Understanding buoyancy and pressure isn't just background knowledge, it's directly useful. Buoyancy affects how much effort duck diving takes to get below the surface, and pressure changes are exactly why equalizing techniques exist. See <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r19">Duck Diving Basics</a> for how these ideas apply in practice.</p>
          `,
