      estimated_reading_time: "3-4 minutes",
      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Light, Vision &amp; Sound Underwater</h2>
            <p>Water changes the way you see and hear in ways that can catch new snorkelers off guard. Understanding why helps you interpret what you're experiencing rather than wondering if something's wrong with your gear.</p>
            <h2>How Light Changes Underwater</h2>
            <p>As light travels through water, different wavelengths are absorbed at different rates. Warm colors like red and orange fade first, even at fairly shallow depths, which is why the underwater world often looks more blue and green than it would in a photo taken with flash or under bright surface light.</p>
            <p>Light also bends as it passes from air into water, an effect called refraction. Because your mask creates an air pocket in front of your eyes, this bending makes objects appear larger and closer than they actually are. This is a normal, expected effect of how mask optics work, not a sign of a problem with your vision or your gear.</p>
            <h2>How Sound Changes Underwater</h2>
            <p>Sound travels roughly four times faster through water than through air. That might sound like it would make sounds easier to locate, but the opposite is often true: because sound reaches both ears so quickly underwater, it becomes harder to tell exactly which direction it's coming from. This is worth knowing if you're relying on sound for communication or awareness while snorkeling with others.</p>
            <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
            <p>Knowing that colors fade, objects appear closer than they are, and sound is hard to localize helps you snorkel with better judgment. It also explains a common observation: someone who needs vision correction may notice these effects differently, which is part of why lens selection for prescription masks is its own topic. See <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r31">Prescription Masks: Nearsightedness, Farsightedness &amp; Lens Selection</a> for that buying decision.</p>
          `,
