      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Equalizing Basics</h2>
            <p>As you descend even a short distance below the surface, pressure builds on the airspaces in your ears and sinuses. Equalizing relieves that pressure so you stay comfortable and avoid injury. See <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r24">Buoyancy, Pressure &amp; Airspaces</a> for the science behind why this happens.</p>
            <h2>Why Equalizing Matters</h2>
            <p>Without equalizing, increasing pressure on your middle ear and sinuses can cause discomfort, pain, and in more serious cases injury known as barotrauma. The fix is straightforward: adjust the pressure in these airspaces to match the surrounding water pressure as you go down.</p>
            <h2>When to Start</h2>
            <p>Begin equalizing early, at the first sign of pressure, rather than waiting until it's uncomfortable. Even shallow depths of half a meter or so can be enough to notice the effect. Equalizing little and often as you descend is more comfortable than trying to catch up after the fact.</p>
            <h2>Common Equalizing Methods</h2>
            <p><strong>Valsalva maneuver:</strong> Pinch your nose closed, close your mouth, and gently blow out through your nose. This helps open the Eustachian tube and relieve pressure in the middle ear.</p>
            <p><strong>Frenzel maneuver:</strong> Pinch your nose as with the Valsalva method, but instead of blowing forcefully, make a "K" sound while trying to push air through your nose. Many people find this a more controlled alternative.</p>
            <p><strong>Jaw wiggle:</strong> With your mouth closed, move your jaw side to side and back and forth. This movement alone can help open the Eustachian tube.</p>
            <p><strong>Yawn method:</strong> Simulate a yawn by opening your mouth wide and stretching your jaw. Like the jaw wiggle, this can help open the Eustachian tube without needing to pinch your nose.</p>
            <h2>Tips for Comfortable Equalizing</h2>
            <ul>
              <li>Never force it. If a method isn't working, forcing it can cause discomfort or injury.</li>
              <li>You should feel gentle relief in both ears after equalizing, not strain or pain.</li>
              <li>If you're having difficulty, ascend slightly and try again rather than pushing further down.</li>
              <li>Clearing your nose before descending can make equalizing easier by keeping your nasal passages clear.</li>
            </ul>
          `,
