      lesson_body_html: `
            <h2>Marine Life Basics</h2>
            <p>You don't need to be a marine biologist to enjoy identifying what you see while snorkeling. Knowing a few broad groups gives you a helpful starting point for making sense of the wildlife around you.</p>
            <h2>Cartilaginous Fish</h2>
            <p>Sharks and rays fall into this group, named for skeletons made of cartilage rather than bone. Look for their sleek, streamlined bodies, the graceful gliding motion of rays, and the swift, purposeful movement of sharks.</p>
            <h2>Bony Fish</h2>
            <p>This is the most diverse group of fish in the ocean, with skeletons made of bone and a swim bladder that helps them control buoyancy. Scales, fins, and gills are the easiest features to spot when identifying bony fish while snorkeling.</p>
            <h2>Invertebrates</h2>
            <p>Invertebrates have no backbone and come in an enormous range of shapes, sizes, and colors, from sea stars and sea urchins to jellyfish and coral polyps. Taking time to look closely into the nooks and crannies of a reef often reveals invertebrates that are easy to miss at a glance.</p>
            <h2>Marine Mammals</h2>
            <p>Dolphins, whales, and seals are warm-blooded animals that have adapted fully to ocean life. Look for signs like dorsal fins, flippers, or a spout of water as they surface to breathe. Keep a respectful distance and observe quietly, getting too close can disturb their natural behavior.</p>
            <h2>Watching Responsibly</h2>
            <p>Identifying marine life is more rewarding when you observe without disturbing it. Keeping your distance, moving calmly, and letting animals behave naturally around you leads to better sightings and less stress on the wildlife. See <a class="learning-inline-reference" href="#preview-r30">Coral, Conservation and Responsible Behavior</a> for more on responsible underwater behavior.</p>
          `,
